# reconcile — fail
#
# Failing to make peace, and being attacked for it: the checks pass, the dice do not, and the
# enemy takes the opening.
#
# --npc-hostile is what makes this reachable: reconcile applies only to an enemy (or an annoying
# acquaintance), and unlike appease it needs somebody who can SPEAK — so --spawn-beast does not help,
# a wolf cannot be reconciled with. Earning a human enemy in script means committing a crime, being
# caught, losing the confrontation and walking out of the fight, which would make this a test of all
# of that instead.
#
# --auto-dialogue settles the reconcile tree this verb opens; the trees are covered separately by
# cli/_systems/dialogue_*.cli.
#
# FLAGS: --playground --skip-childhood --debug --seed 42 --no-encounters --auto-dialogue --npc-hostile --location-type test --location-id 0 --start-area "Test Hall" --period dawn --observe-only "brewer"

wait
click menu New
wait mode ProtagonistCreation 20
click continue
wait mode WorldView 60
travel here
wait mode LocationInteraction 60

strategy fail-dice
goal reconcile
advance 12 180
regions

click keyword 0
choose 0
choose 0
advance 12 180
regions

click action 0
choose 0
advance 12 180
click continue

# Drain the outcome preview: the fight is queued as a pending outcome and only starts once that box
# is committed.
advance 12 180

# The fight, not an outcome banner. A failed reconciliation with somebody who counts you an enemy and
# is standing right there is an opportunity attack (ProximityModel — effective-Visual threat +
# failure ⇒ fight), so no outcome block is ever written. Same shape as cli/appease/fail.cli.
wait mode Fighting 60

# What makes this a reconcile test rather than an any-verb test.
expect-verb reconcile
quit
